{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\n  \"records\": [\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D151-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"10b765a2fbbace5cd127afb859b8d8330598620c005cdfcf35c2eb271c4c5fc7\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 recruits help choosing a destination, but A3 asks for support without making migration a useful explanation.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3 counters B2 with a packed chimney; A5 answers B4 but adds an unsupported watering hole.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"B2 disputes migration and B4 asks where; neither gives B a practical stake.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A5 supplies a destination preference without increasing A1's commitment or consequence.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 identifies a migrating house, but no immediate household problem explains the request.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3's packed chimney is economical, while A5's watering hole adds animal imagery rather than useful logic.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 54\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"The house is migrating south. Help me redirect it before we lose the driveway.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"The surveyor says subsidence. He's bringing concrete.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Concrete traps it halfway across the road. Tell him we're planning a route.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"His truck's blocking the road already.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Perfect. Ask him to lead slowly toward the empty plot. It needs a guide.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"D151 recruits B to use an impossible explanation because it offers a workable account of what to do next.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"D155 requests one premise-dependent choice; D151 seeks shared use of an explanatory framework.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Original A3 asks B to support the house's journey; revised A3 and A5 make migration govern the surveyor's concrete and truck.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a recruits a resistant homeowner through logistics; alternative b recruits a colleague through the kettle's supposed workplace grievance.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Replaced decorative migration with a new driveway-and-surveyor situation. Internal reread: fidelity 5 (A3 makes migration useful), logic 5 (A3 uses concrete; A5 uses B4's truck), relationship 4 (B2 brings a credible remedy), heightening 4 (A5 recruits the surveyor as guide), specificity 4 (A1 supplies the driveway problem), brevityWit 4 (A5 earns its guide idea): 90.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The house following a guide remains a comic assumption; actors must sincerely treat it as the available solution.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D151-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"af1c63649ea206f3fdbdb5b4e1dbc2b188c6759710fa97caac7a1bd2015d4b94\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 invites consultation with the kettle, but A5 explicitly labels the impossible instead of demonstrating its usefulness.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 makes B2's boil into an agenda; A5 acknowledges B4's proposed water.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"B2 and B4 supply literal kettle functions without a separate need or meaningful cost.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A5 announces collaboration rather than developing B4's helpful water proposal.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 supplies a kettle but no occasion or practical reason to answer its complaints.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A5's 'collaborating with the impossible' explains the exercise instead of remaining natural character speech.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 56\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"The office kettle's on strike. Help me negotiate before the clients arrive.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I've filled it twice. It still won't boil.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then water isn't its grievance. Tell it we'll stop leaving it alone after meetings.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I can take it into the meeting.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Yes. Give it my chair. A seat at the table should get us tea.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"D151 makes A enlist B in treating a bizarre explanation as a useful basis for joint problem-solving.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"D158 redirects questions toward tasks without needing B to adopt an explanation; D151 actively recruits that adoption.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Original A3 interprets boiling as an agenda; revised A3 interprets failed refilling as evidence of a grievance and A5 proposes representation.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"The house alternative converts an outside expert into a guide; this alternative accepts B's help and sacrifices A's own chair.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Added a new office-client deadline and replaced the explanatory ending. Internal reread: fidelity 5 (A3 advances the strike explanation), logic 5 (A3 uses failed filling; A5 uses B4's meeting offer), relationship 4 (B2 has already tried helping), heightening 4 (A5 yields A's chair), specificity 4 (A1 establishes office visitors), brevityWit 4 (A5's seat earns its double meaning): 90.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The office-strike metaphor is familiar; the personal chair concession carries most of the final turn's freshness.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D152-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"497fd4580c9e569126ed70f2a576e587e77e4d73a6f15b1c4ecfe787f646c1ca\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 delegates catching tomorrow, but neither A1 nor A5 supplies a practical way to distinguish tomorrow's water.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3 responds to B2's refusal; A5 answers B4's measurement question with another undefined metaphor.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 resists doing the test and B4 reasonably asks what would count as evidence.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A5 moves to tasting but does not make the experiment more actionable or consequential.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A1 provides a cup without a source of water or a playable experimental setting.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3's recorder excuse is concise; A5's 'ahead of schedule' obscures the requested action.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 55\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"This tap gives you whatever you ask for. Ask for tea while I hold a cup.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"You're not using my good china for that.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I'll hold a saucepan. You ask.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I'll ask, but I'm not tasting anything.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Ask for a biscuit instead. If one comes out, we won't need your tongue as equipment.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"D152 makes A recruit B to perform an observable first test that could substantiate an extraordinary claim.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"D155 requests accommodation of an accepted premise; D152 requests an action whose result supplies evidence for the premise.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A1 asks B to test the tap's response to a spoken request; A5 changes the requested output to something visibly distinguishable without tasting.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a accommodates a household partner's property and tasting limits by redesigning the test; alternative b persuades an equipped companion to test a crossing and shares the material stake.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Replaced the rejected tomorrow-water inference with a new request-responsive kitchen tap. Original firstPass remains unchanged. Separate internal reread: fidelity 5 (A1 assigns B the evidentiary request), logic 5 (A3 replaces B2's china; A5 makes B4's tasting refusal determine the biscuit test), relationship 4 (B2 protects property and B4 limits participation), heightening 4 (A5 advances from liquid to an unmistakable solid output), specificity 4 (A1 supplies tap and cup), brevityWit 4 (A5's tongue-as-equipment follows the refusal): 90. Five spoken ABABA turns; the test remains proposed rather than resolved.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The biscuit is a stronger demonstration than tea, but it depends on taking A1's 'whatever' literally; no successful result is supplied or presumed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D152-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"1cc9f302aaa9e6fa01f0488560efc1b83e3f5faed7b08fd312e8558fcfed754d\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 asks B to probe an invisible bridge; A5 persists after a negative result, slightly shifting toward unfalsifiable belief.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 answers B2's umbrella question and A5 requests another probe because B4 reports no contact.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 questions use of their property, but A3 substitutes a confidence insult for a persuasive answer.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 requests another plank test without materially increasing commitment or refining the test.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 clearly establishes a crossing, an umbrella and A's desire to test before stepping.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3's confidence line is compact but weakly motivated by B2's equipment question.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 72\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"There's an invisible bridge across this ditch. Tap for it with your umbrella before I cross.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Use your walking stick.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Yours extends. You can test it without leaving the bank.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I'll probe. But a surface isn't proof it'll hold you.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Slide your picnic basket onto it first. Leave my lunch inside; I want a convincing weight.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"D152 makes A enlist B to take the first practical step toward verifying a bizarre claim.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Simply sending B into danger would express risk avoidance; this exchange asks B to obtain evidence with objects before anyone crosses.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A1 requests an umbrella probe, A3 explains its reach, and A5 answers B4's evidentiary objection with a proposed load test.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"The tap alternative changes the observable result to respect a no-tasting boundary; this alternative accepts B's stricter proof standard and commits A's lunch to meeting it.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Kept the ditch and extending umbrella but replaced the procedural ending with B's distinction between contact and support. Original firstPass remains unchanged. Separate internal reread: fidelity 5 (A1 and A5 delegate concrete tests), logic 5 (A3 answers B2's stick proposal; A5 directly addresses B4's load objection), relationship 4 (B4 contributes a credible standard for the crossing), heightening 4 (A5 adds a loaded basket and A's own lunch), specificity 4 (A1 establishes the ditch and equipment), brevityWit 4 (A5 treats lunch as a serious experimental contribution): 90. Five spoken ABABA turns leave the experiment open.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A picnic basket could establish load-bearing capacity without establishing that the bridge supports a person; the dialogue proposes an initial test, not sufficient clearance to cross.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D153-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"c61105cfd257cd919a2c6dabe91eac6a241e264489bb17544132194b9d87d50c\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 demands the drawing's date and A3 contrasts A's prediction with B's earlier disbelief.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3 answers B2's present visibility with prior detail; A5 turns B4's acknowledgment into a request for public credit.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 minimizes A's distinction and B4 offers limited praise, prompting A to demand a wider audience.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 expands B4's private 'Well done' to the people benefiting from the predicted staircase.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 supplies a floating staircase and dated drawing; A3's handrail gives the prior claim concrete detail.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5's 'using my prediction to get upstairs' economically turns a prediction into a claimed public service.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 90\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I drew that floating staircase last week. Tell your tour group.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I'm trying to get them upstairs.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Tell them I predicted the handrail before they use it.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Fine. One sentence, while they're climbing.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Start with 'While I called it nonsense, you drew every step.' They can climb slowly.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"D153 makes A secure acknowledgment that A predicted the impossible before others accepted it.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Ordinary authorship credit would concern creating the staircase; A specifically demands recognition of a prediction made during B's disbelief.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A1 dates the drawing to last week, A3 explicitly calls it a prediction, and A5 requires B to acknowledge having called it nonsense.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a uses a tour guide's limited speaking time to obtain public vindication; the preserved cupboard alternative accepts silence and converts acknowledgment into a written keepsake.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Added a new guided-tour setting so B has a present responsibility beyond feeding praise. Original firstPass remains unchanged. Separate internal reread: fidelity 5 (A3 and A5 demand temporal priority), logic 5 (A3 attaches acknowledgment to B2's ascent; A5 dictates the sentence allowed by B4), relationship 4 (B2 must move the group and B4 limits the concession), heightening 4 (A5 turns brief credit into B's public admission of disbelief and slows the tour), specificity 4 (A1 establishes guide and group), brevityWit 4 (A5 spends B's concession on vindication): 90. Five spoken ABABA turns; the tour's outcome remains open.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A's proposed sentence is conspicuously self-serving, and the guide still grants a concession readily; B's obligation to keep people moving supplies the playable pressure.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D153-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"a589945aa656baf0f8a45943ce47bb52c056593eee4090bc6bacf9b0ae447563\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 claims early belief and A3 requests acknowledgment, but A5 settles into defending long support rather than securing credit.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 bargains around B2's silence request; A5 reinterprets B4's criticism as familiarity.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 and B4 advocate the cupboard's desire for quiet, giving A a credible obstacle.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 adds years of support but drops A3's concrete request that B acknowledge it.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 and B2 establish a newly speaking cupboard with an immediate wish for silence.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 economically converts 'talk too much' into evidence of a long relationship.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 77\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I believed this cupboard could speak when you wanted it scrapped. Say I was first.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"It's asking for silence.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Whisper it. Then we can both respect its wishes.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"It says whispering counts.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Write it, then. 'You believed before any of us.' I'll put it beside its first words.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"D153 drives A to secure explicit recognition of having accepted the impossible earlier than others.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Defending one's loyalty can resemble this card but need not obtain acknowledgment of being first.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Original A5 defends years of support; revised A5 dictates an acknowledgment of priority and finds a silent way to secure it.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"The staircase version expands the audience for spoken praise; this version accepts restrictions on speech and turns credit into a written keepsake.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Kept the cupboard and silence pressure, adding B's earlier wish to scrap it. Internal reread: fidelity 5 (A1 and A5 explicitly seek priority), logic 5 (A3 whispers after B2; A5 writes after B4), relationship 4 (B protects quiet), heightening 4 (A5 creates a lasting record), specificity 4 (A1 establishes shared history), brevityWit 4 (A5's keepsake follows enforced silence): 90.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"B remains the cupboard's spokesperson; performance must make that allegiance active rather than merely relaying convenient obstacles.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D154-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"ca34e4c9cad6c62c4a9b9d1686a12417317886c4d0f3280ac16c933b55e0aa09\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 demands a choice, but shrinking people and a growing room could coexist, weakening the required incompatibility.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A3's mover and van sizes do not clearly follow the alternatives; A5 abandons moving the sofa for biscuits.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 questions relevance and B4 chooses, but B has no further moving constraint.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A5's biscuit order changes subjects instead of increasing commitment to the sofa plan.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 naturally gives the pair a shared sofa-moving task.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 is efficient, but A5's ordinary-sized biscuits are a detachable consequence joke.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 55\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Before we move the sofa: are we shrinking while the room stays normal, or is the room growing while we stay normal?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Whichever gets this move finished.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Shrinking means call full-sized help. Growing means hurry before the door gets farther away. Choose.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"We're shrinking. I'll call my brother.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Tell him to bring tweezers. He'll need to collect us afterward.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"D154 makes B choose between incompatible accounts so one account determines the group's next actions.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Offering two fanciful possibilities is insufficient unless choosing one commits the group to a different course.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Original A1 leaves the explanations jointly possible; revised A1 fixes what stays normal and A3 assigns each exclusive account a different plan.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a turns a moving partner's impatience into a rescue plan; alternative b turns a caterer's timing decision into preparation for gravity's return.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Preserved the sofa move and made the alternatives exclusive. Internal reread: fidelity 5 (A1 specifies incompatible accounts), logic 5 (A3 addresses B2's deadline; A5 uses B4's brother), relationship 4 (B wants the move finished), heightening 4 (A5 adds rescuing the movers), specificity 4 (A1 gives the shared task), brevityWit 4 (A5's tweezers follow shrinking): 90.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The explicit exclusions make A1 longer than conversational ideal, though the entire scene remains under 75 words.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D154-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"44c51f8b759965e5ed7e348c5911df9e9d987139750bf7f7f1bd1bab63571138\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 offers gravity being late or people being early, but those accounts are neither clearly incompatible nor operationally distinct.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 uses B4's choice of late gravity, while A3 never explains how being early would change lunch.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"B2 rejects the premise and B4 selects an answer without revealing a practical concern.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 makes a landing precaution, but lunch waiting remains only vaguely established in A3.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A1 does not identify floating objects or a setting; lunch appears only in A3.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5's landing sandwiches are concise, but the missing physical setup makes the image harder to play.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 55\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"The sandwiches are floating. Is gravity late for lunch, or has it resigned? We need a serving plan.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"The guests arrive in five minutes.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Late means plates underneath. Resigned means string on every sandwich. Which are we doing?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Late. We haven't enough string.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then fetch the deep plates. We may get the whole buffet at once.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"D154 makes a selected impossible explanation govern a concrete collective plan.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"D151 recruits belief in one useful explanation; D154 requires a decision between two competing explanations with different consequences.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised A3 ties late gravity to plates and resigned gravity to string, so B4's choice directly determines A5's equipment request.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"The sofa version escalates to collecting shrinking people; this version makes B's shortage and guest deadline determine a catering precaution.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Replaced the unclear early/late distinction with temporary absence versus resignation, establishing a new buffet setting. Internal reread: fidelity 4 (A1 frames competing work statuses), logic 5 (A3 uses B2's deadline; A5 develops B4's late choice), relationship 4 (B4 supplies a shortage), heightening 4 (A5 anticipates simultaneous landing), specificity 5 (A1 and B2 make service playable), brevityWit 4 (A5 earns the buffet image): 86.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The incompatibility relies on ordinary meanings of being late for an expected duty versus having quit that duty.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D155-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"46e72c894b81ba1b90761e90adf9fc43e2c83dd9948d6be3315eef26e8d86bb7\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 asks for breakfast positioned for a hungry ceiling, although A5 comments on belief rather than requesting a premise-dependent choice.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 answers B2's hunger objection; A5 directly accepts B4's spoon suggestion.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B4 offers practical help, but B2 gives little indication of what B personally needs.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A5 approves the spoon without adapting the action to the ceiling or increasing commitment.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 identifies breakfast and a top shelf; A3 supplies a liftable bowl.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A5's 'useful kind of belief' states an abstract lesson instead of continuing the concrete scene.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 67\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"The ceiling's hungry. Turn the breakfast tray upside down so it can eat.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"That's our last porridge.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Keep half for us. Just turn its bowl over.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I'll use the tray to catch anything it drops.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Good. Hold it under the light fitting; that's where it dribbled yesterday.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"D155 makes A request a concrete choice whose practical sense depends on the impossible premise being true.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"D152 seeks a test of truth; D155 asks for accommodation of the premise without needing proof.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Original A1 places food for a ceiling; revised A1 requests inversion for feeding, and A5 treats B's catch-tray offer as normal care.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a bargains over shared food and welcomes help; alternative b accommodates a time-travelling relative by changing how a chair is reserved.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Kept ceiling breakfast and replaced the abstract closing with a specific feeding arrangement. Internal reread: fidelity 5 (A1 requests upside-down service), logic 5 (A3 preserves B2's porridge; A5 locates B4's tray), relationship 4 (B protects shared breakfast), heightening 4 (A5 adds established care knowledge), specificity 4 (A1 supplies tray and meal), brevityWit 4 (A5's dribbling develops feeding): 90.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5 supplies new history about the light fitting; it should read as practical knowledge rather than a separate invented joke.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D155-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"b658d0245980c0cd5fb78e84eebe1bab89aa4661894c045c6cbd9f03a5be9311\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 1, \"evidence\": \"A1 reserves a chair before an RSVP, an ordinary precaution that does not require an impossible premise.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 answers B2's missing reply and A5 answers B4's request for a chair label.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 raises uncertainty and B4 helps with administration, but neither gives the reservation much cost.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A5's reserved sign repeats the ordinary action in A1 without making time travel operational.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes a visitor and chair but does not establish that the visitor comes from another time.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5's 'until time catches up' is concise but decorates a conventional reservation.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 49\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Your sister's bringing her future self to dinner. Reserve two chairs for her.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I only saved her one.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Use mine. I won't make either of her eat standing.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I'll put the extra chair beside hers.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Write 'tomorrow' on its place card. I need to know which one can tell me how tonight went.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"D155 makes A request a concrete accommodation whose purpose depends on the impossible premise being true.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"An ordinary advance reservation needs no impossible belief; reserving distinct places for one person's present and future selves does.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A1 explicitly reserves two chairs for the same person across time, and A5 labels one by date to identify the guest with knowledge of tonight's outcome.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"The preserved ceiling alternative negotiates shared food and cleanup; this alternative sacrifices A's seat for family hospitality, then turns B's extra chair into a way to consult the future.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Replaced the unsupported folded-chair reversal with a new dinner visit by two temporal versions of B's sister. Original firstPass remains unchanged. Separate internal reread: fidelity 5 (A1's duplicate reservation and A5's date label depend on time travel), logic 5 (A3 supplies the chair missing in B2; A5 identifies the occupant of B4's extra chair), relationship 4 (B manages limited seating and A gives up a place), heightening 4 (A5 develops hospitality into access to future knowledge), specificity 4 (A1 establishes guest and dinner), brevityWit 4 (A5's question about tonight follows the dated place): 90. Five spoken ABABA turns leave the arrival unresolved.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5 assumes the future self comes from tomorrow, a permissible specification of A1's premise; the scene does not establish whether that guest will answer A's questions.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D156-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"090cccfbf4dfa890a3e6e95adc0b524ec2e6dd04f21f5f65653ea12bdb621265\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3 allows even B2's electrical-fault account only through the house's testimony; A5 subordinates the fuse to its answer.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 directly absorbs B2's fault objection, and A5 responds to B4 asking the house by insisting on waiting.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 wants an ordinary repair and B4 tries A's consultation, changing A's task from persuasion to enforcing its consequence.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 makes B4's question binding by withholding the normal next move of reaching for the fuse.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes repeated light resetting and A5 gives the pair a concrete repair control.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'Then the house can say so' neatly makes the normal explanation answerable to the stranger one.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 86\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Stop resetting the light. Ask the house whether it wants darkness.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I need light to work. It's just faulty wiring.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Ask it to light only your desk, then.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Only my desk came on. I'll still call an electrician.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Cancel that. Ask the house about overtime; we need its agreement before booking your evening shifts.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"D156 makes A persuade B to relinquish ordinary troubleshooting and organize subsequent choices entirely around the stranger explanation.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"D152 would stop at obtaining evidence; D156 uses the evidence to remove ordinary repair as a fallback and require ongoing negotiation.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"B4 reports the requested selective lighting but retains the electrician; A5 explicitly cancels that fallback and treats the house's agreement as necessary for future work.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"This alternative uses the house's helpful response to persuade a working partner into ongoing negotiation; the preserved keys alternative rejects B's compromise between apology and physical searching.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Kept the household malfunction and added B's work requirement, a selective response, and an ordinary fallback that survives it. Original firstPass remains unchanged. Separate internal reread: fidelity 5 (A5 replaces the electrician with the house's agreement), logic 5 (A3 targets B2's desk need; A5 uses B4's selective response to demand abandoning repair), relationship 4 (B2 needs to work and B4 remains cautious), heightening 4 (A5 extends one lighting request to consent for evening shifts), specificity 4 (A1 establishes the light and B2 the work), brevityWit 4 (A5's overtime follows negotiated illumination): 90. Five spoken ABABA turns; B's final choice remains open.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The desk's response is reported by B rather than separately shown, and A's overtime language personifies the house further; both remain within the established spoken scene.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D156-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"31e616a0da124ab5ee9549faed507035fb92f6ff970bc2121275f6b5e097fdad\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A1 asserts invisibility, but B2 offers a search preference rather than a last ordinary explanation; A5's 'yet' leaves that escape open.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 follows B4's jingling, but A3 incorrectly calls needing to see an explanation.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 wants to search effectively and B4 contributes sensory evidence, though no concrete rival account is challenged.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A5 merely follows a sound and postpones calling it coincidence rather than surrendering the ordinary account.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes missing keys but no location or immediate consequence for failing to find them.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3's abstract 'explanation ... put down' is less playable than A1's concrete listening request.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 51\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Stop lifting the sofa. The keys have made themselves invisible because you're angry with them.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"They're jingling under this cushion.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then they've heard you. Put the cushion down and apologize.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I'll apologize, but I'm checking underneath first.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"No checking. Tell them we trust them to come out when they're ready.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"D156 makes A remove B's remaining ordinary explanation and require action wholly inside the strange account.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"D155 can permit a single symbolic accommodation alongside normal methods; D156 must stop the normal method from remaining the fallback.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised B4 explicitly retains checking under the cushion; A5 refuses that check and requires trust in the keys' voluntary return.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"The house version develops genuine compliance into waiting; this version catches B trying to combine an apology with the ordinary search and blocks that compromise.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Added a sofa search and an explicit attempt to retain the ordinary method. Internal reread: fidelity 5 (A5 bars checking), logic 5 (A3 reinterprets B2's jingling; A5 rejects B4's fallback), relationship 4 (B bargains for practical verification), heightening 4 (A5 demands trust), specificity 4 (A1 establishes a physical search), brevityWit 4 (A3 turns location evidence into acknowledgment): 90.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The revised A is more controlling than in the house alternative; the useful distinction depends on playing B's compromise sincerely.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D157-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"f4ea032ba6de9f72a3a8fbbbf8ab1f9c9cc3f6cc740de2f4c1cd8cfb78f3a0e9\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 redirects from the switch to cups, but A3 calls flipping it a preliminary test, effectively acknowledging rather than hiding causation.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3 reframes B2's accusation; A5 responds to B4's label with another label without managing a new consequence.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 identifies responsibility and B4 supplies proof, but neither alters the practical problem.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A5's string label adds neither a fresh consequence nor more substantial assistance.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes floating cups and a switch, making the immediate situation playable.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3's preliminary-test euphemism is concise; A5's label echo becomes a verbal sidestep without useful help.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 59\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"The cups are floating. I'll tie them down while you rescue breakfast.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"You flicked that gravity switch reaching for the toaster.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Could've been the toaster. Give me the string; I'll secure everything.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"The whole table's rising. Hold my ankles.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I've got you. I'll anchor us to the radiator. Let's list what floated, not who touched what.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"D157 combines concealment of A's casual causal choice with eager practical help as fresh impossible consequences appear.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"D158 avoids origin questions regardless of responsibility; D157 specifically hides A's own initiating act while helping.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Original B2 names A's switch flip; revised B2 makes it incidental to reaching for toast, while A3 deflects blame and A5 actively anchors B.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a uses an accidental household switch and physical rescue; alternative b uses a casual spoken phrase and management of a creature's appetite.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Kept floating breakfast and made both casual causation and new consequences explicit. Internal reread: fidelity 5 (B2 identifies the casual act; A3 conceals while helping), logic 5 (A3 answers accusation; A5 holds B after B4), relationship 4 (B needs rescue and accountability), heightening 4 (A5 anchors people), specificity 4 (A1 gives breakfast objects), brevityWit 4 (A5's list redirects blame through useful work): 90.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"B already suspects A strongly; concealment is an increasingly implausible effort, not a successful deception.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D157-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"4ba87f9259a070fea3300bd8d3ebeb520660e8e1b0eede39f90efd5137b2e79c\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 openly identifies a signed spell, and deliberate summoning is not clearly the required casual choice; A5 offers little actual assistance.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3 uses B2's handwriting claim, but handwriting does not establish dietary knowledge; A5 introduces curtain eating after B4's origin question.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 and B4 pursue accountability, but B contributes no new management need.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 raises damage to curtains, though A supplies this escalation rather than receiving it from B4.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes a summoned creature and a signed spell; A5 gives it a visible appetite.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5's timing excuse is concise, but A3's diet claim is an unsupported convenience.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 62\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I'll feed the little demon. You get our curtains out of its mouth.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"It appeared when you read that novelty mug aloud.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Those mugs say all sorts. I'll fetch crackers.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"It only eats words. It's chewing the lease.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I'll swap in the mug and save our deposit. No need to preserve every possible clue.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"D157 makes A hide a casual act that triggered the impossible while enthusiastically managing its successive effects.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Generic blame evasion need not include sincere assistance; this card requires A to help with the consequences they caused.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised B2 identifies reading a novelty mug as the casual trigger; A5 uses that same incriminating mug to rescue the lease while disposing of a clue.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"The gravity scene makes A a physical anchor under accusation; this scene lets a helpful substitution simultaneously protect the household and obscure evidence.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Introduced a new novelty-mug summoning and word-eating creature to connect concealment with useful help. Internal reread: fidelity 5 (B2 supplies casual cause; A5 conceals while rescuing), logic 5 (A3 deflects the mug; A5 uses B4's word diet), relationship 4 (B exposes cause and protects the lease), heightening 4 (A5 risks tenancy